#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175754 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "acac66ab-ff2a-5727-8a2c-9cce3e18806c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9036" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2xy - x^2 - y^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x - y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(x-y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x - y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  =  z^2 + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = z^2 + 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175755 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "37a41ca0-2205-5368-91ec-d9a4c1a309d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9038" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{1 - x - y}{x + y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = 2x + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-(x+y)}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{1-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =x+K \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 = 2x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y) = (x+y)^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0640566c-2a53-5bfa-9000-3c75aababe7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331180} {:answer/id #uuid "b2259e1a-fd12-54bb-9b79-25dc3270bc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331181} {:answer/id #uuid "ad48cf59-082d-5094-a471-77cff00eb457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331182} {:answer/id #uuid "82f4c438-bbc4-54f8-a631-f7b0e02e9a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331183} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0ad70-5f05-59b9-ae1a-f778f211c4d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82f4c438-bbc4-54f8-a631-f7b0e02e9a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331183} :answer-field/id #uuid "573c3678-f514-54e8-affa-eef87ad08a37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a70f4011-4095-5c5c-9618-7d0bafab75e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-107815" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+y}{3-2x-y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+y}{3-(2x+y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{z}{3-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6-z}{3-z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{3-z}{6-z})\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (\\frac{z-3}{z-6})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{3}{z-6}+1)\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z-6|+z & =x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6|+2x+y & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6| & =-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{3}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=2x+y-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3e6e875-21f0-5db7-926f-b027db4d79ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331187} {:answer/id #uuid "812036a8-b2fd-5a87-ad19-5148886333bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331188} {:answer/id #uuid "12777a36-fe4e-5d4d-ab33-61327efc0292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331189} {:answer/id #uuid "533b4fcb-59c7-564b-9bdc-f81850a3364e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331190} {:answer/id #uuid "63808a1f-fa0c-5112-ac0b-776a99647c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "812036a8-b2fd-5a87-ad19-5148886333bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331188} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e98113-d8bb-5253-a144-550249df0549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fec4fdaf-56a7-522b-8d95-ea2485a21ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-108736" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}+6xy+3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3(x+y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3{z}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3{z}^{2}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39463677-e0fe-5670-9c28-a1c02cd176ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡(4z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331194} {:answer/id #uuid "cc59e81a-787b-582a-bae8-e9aa3eff3d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331195} {:answer/id #uuid "4edc0619-fbb9-5c0c-8c4f-68d37673e481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡z+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331196} {:answer/id #uuid "cfcfae2a-9750-5ccd-a7d3-c87911573225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡z-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331197} {:answer/id #uuid "3c342868-c58a-5f7c-9485-faf3d4ac62b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡(4z)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc59e81a-787b-582a-bae8-e9aa3eff3d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331195} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3f770b-77a0-5017-b9d5-072b6aeb9cce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "993f5c75-e412-5bbe-b9e0-a96c54f0d801" :question/math-academy-id "q-108744" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(4x+4y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}⁡(4(x+y)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eacb3cb-8d78-5277-b21b-e3d021fa4a39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331201} {:answer/id #uuid "c76994ff-62db-5ff1-9130-a50c571865e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331202} {:answer/id #uuid "551cda62-af71-5acf-a5ae-c002ff40316b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z+1}{z+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331203} {:answer/id #uuid "fdedee0c-1f00-594a-8d9a-a7494939a354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331204} {:answer/id #uuid "4133446b-1911-5d42-bd15-9d447d3f7969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4z+1}{6z+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eacb3cb-8d78-5277-b21b-e3d021fa4a39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331201} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47bc8b2-5c24-5d43-b309-eb36e7977cab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd5f84f3-55db-5db3-a4c1-830365d71688" :question/math-academy-id "q-108748" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{8x-12y+1}{12x-18y+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-3y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4(2x-3y)+1}{6(2x-3y)+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-3y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{4z+1}{6z+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0b7c68f-6ff6-592a-889c-d26bcf2c8916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2z-3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331208} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea87074-c1c9-5565-9490-b3010972ee3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-z}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331209} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc5feef-0612-5411-9a8e-137ba3e0dfaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{3(z+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331210} {:answer/id #uuid "33b1fc89-9cac-500c-8d4b-8d70bd353cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{z+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331211} {:answer/id #uuid "94bd4a66-6cd4-5dbc-99bd-4162efe47de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94bd4a66-6cd4-5dbc-99bd-4162efe47de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331212} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f30a456-3ee5-5f85-9091-d5e0a2cb5440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a09417b-e997-5f18-b23e-c683beaef143" :question/math-academy-id "q-108751" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2+y-x}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-(x-y)}{x-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51144b11-e982-5804-b7d6-42fd9dec3cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331215} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3155ac-ac4c-5ebf-a2d7-1dae9effd319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331216} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac16cd1-564e-5d20-9d0a-dcf4a698fc7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331217} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed1c7ee-eeed-57ab-a167-29699ec78136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331218} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf3dd23-f39d-513d-9d83-6b87f102dd48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+y)^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51144b11-e982-5804-b7d6-42fd9dec3cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331215} :answer-field/id #uuid "41bd10ca-6a61-5336-bf54-f7c45095782c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e665c84-07e3-5a1c-901b-05e7974df036" :question/math-academy-id "q-108775" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+y)^{2}+2(x+y)+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(g(x,y))=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(x+y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =(z+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=(z+1)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z+1) & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+1)=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+y+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964c03f9-4f12-5948-a298-08a51b975ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331222} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d3ec9c-78b0-5791-b714-682eb6d99aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331223} {:answer/id #uuid "f8068d23-a345-5e82-bd49-5acdb07c4a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4z+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331224} {:answer/id #uuid "135d3cfa-26e6-5fa2-ba44-790af0a09445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331225} {:answer/id #uuid "85f421f4-cc2d-5c92-a661-f0d2f1633958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "135d3cfa-26e6-5fa2-ba44-790af0a09445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331225} :answer-field/id #uuid "e182dfc2-6fc7-522d-b540-9c9309d10771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6a39a5a-cfd0-5e93-9a56-a7fc8d75679f" :question/math-academy-id "q-108778" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x+4y-1.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4(x+y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =4z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=4z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=4z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6456dff-d48b-5483-aa90-97d42d55324e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331229} {:answer/id #uuid "5df2b4c2-4517-58cf-8bb1-b025944d9643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331230} {:answer/id #uuid "539356b2-7747-513e-8d28-65488f28e721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331231} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbfeac1-bdbb-5434-8618-336fd693eb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331232} {:answer/id #uuid "6e47a7a4-4764-5915-9e3c-9a0e17081c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6456dff-d48b-5483-aa90-97d42d55324e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331229} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff5d987f-6cc3-573e-bbb5-8fdecb72bcb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c14d9a9-4f15-5867-9804-00f22c44e449" :question/math-academy-id "q-108819" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+5y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=10x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5(x+y))-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =10x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)|=10x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8059d2c7-463e-5c15-85f0-4e94768bb2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331236} {:answer/id #uuid "179be1bc-97f4-5982-a6e7-4df22931b4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-2y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331237} {:answer/id #uuid "a92a7947-9e17-56d5-b1de-6594f2a10e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27x-9y-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331238} {:answer/id #uuid "144ead1d-042b-522a-bb2b-42148622e889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27x-9y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331239} {:answer/id #uuid "7b65f355-09d5-5dd1-8863-12aae6b7a7f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a92a7947-9e17-56d5-b1de-6594f2a10e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27x-9y-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331238} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa58d8ae-2a46-55a3-acf8-e86d2e1104ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01c4d65f-4022-5ad5-a21f-bc193182433e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108857" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{9x-3y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=y+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3(3x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9z-2}{3z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\int (1+\\frac{2}{9z-2})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2|) & =x+K \\\\ z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2| & =3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =3x+C \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=27x-9y-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc1db44-4ab1-5525-946f-87736a667c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331243} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f11252-547e-5fe6-8f82-f2c117c399e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{x-y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331244} {:answer/id #uuid "7033dee8-38d9-51ff-bd5f-d96c2921e7ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331245} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1329ff-1d31-5735-867c-b82a42b2035f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331246} {:answer/id #uuid "8e41fccc-2bbc-59de-8a04-06f7d0136827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e41fccc-2bbc-59de-8a04-06f7d0136827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331247} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d52c54c-c746-55b4-a681-7965b0153475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0979d5db-3469-58fe-a7d2-33d0f82c76a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-149756" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\sqrt{4x-4y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2\\sqrt{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2\\sqrt{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\sqrt{z} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=-\\sqrt{x-y}.\n$$"}]}
